  {"average_elapsed_seconds", "Mean runtime"}
])
```

## QAOA Instance-Level Check

The QAOA validation does not demonstrate scalable quantum advantage. Its role is
narrower: it checks whether exported QUBO instances can be consumed by a QAOA
workflow and recover optimal solutions on small instances.

```elixir
qaoa_instances =
  "results/processed/qaoa_validation_small_matrix/by_instance.csv"
  |> ResultsTable.read_csv()
  |> Enum.map(fn row ->
    %{
      "scenario" => row["scenario"],
      "variables" => row["variable_count"],
      "attempts" => row["attempts"],
      "exact_rate" => ResultsTable.percent(row["exact_match_rate"]),
      "best_gap" => ResultsTable.signed_float(row["best_objective_gap"]),
      "average_elapsed_seconds" => ResultsTable.seconds(row["average_elapsed_seconds"])
    }
  end)

ResultsTable.print_table(qaoa_instances, [
  {"scenario", "Scenario"},
  {"variables", "Variables"},
  {"attempts", "Attempts"},
  {"exact_rate", "Exact rate"},
  {"best_gap", "Best gap"},
  {"average_elapsed_seconds", "Mean runtime"}
])
```

## Technical Reading

```elixir
[
  "QUBO v1 should not be treated as a universal replacement for classical heuristics.",
  "The strongest signal appears in burst_critical/high, where QUBO v1 wins in 90% of seeds.",
  "The targeted matrix supports a hybrid architecture: classical heuristics by default and QUBO as a specialist optimizer.",
  "fairness_starvation is a useful negative result: the current formulation does not dominate when fairness/starvation is the primary pressure.",
  "QAOA validates the QUBO -> variational quantum optimization path on small instances, without claiming scalable quantum advantage."
]
```
